-- title:
--   One round of the deterministic filtering host keeps its invariants and shrinks the weighted active set
-- statement:
--   Let the oracle procedure $p_O$ of $P_0$ solve `oracleTask` with time $T_o$ and need $\nu_o$, and let the procedures of a round (`roundBody`, aggregation, step, final marks, certificates, arg-min, copy) be in place in $P_0\mathbin{+\!\!+}R$ (`RoundProcs`). Let `Env` hold for the all-edges instance $x$ ($n$ vertices, weight bound $U$) with first memory $\mu_0$ and free pointer $f$, and let the memory $\mu$ between two rounds satisfy `FMem` with active marks $act$ and done flags $D$, the invariant `Inv1` (a done pair has a witness $c$ with $w(a,b)+w(b,c)+w(a,c)=0$) and `Inv2` (an active candidate of a pair that is not done, whose block contains a witness, is marked).
--
--   Then the round, called at depth $d\le d_0+2$ with the step arguments, ends within `tRound To n U` steps, and the new memory satisfies `FMem`, `Inv1`, `Inv2` for some new $act'$, $D'$ with
--   $$Q^{r}\cdot|A'|\le (2h)^{r}\cdot s\cdot|A|,$$
--   where $|A|$ is the number of active candidates of pairs that are not done (`cardA` of `eff`), $Q$ the size of the prime pool in $[R,2R)$, $r$ the number of steps per round, $h$ the bound on the number of pool primes dividing a nonzero triangle sum, and $s$ the block size (Lemma 3.1).
